Start the SoC watchdog at driver probe, not only when the bootloader left it
running.

THE PROBLEM
-----------
The surya hangs on boot ~1 out of 4 times, and the most frequent mode kills the
kernel BEFORE the USB gadget shows up (around 18 s). In that window there is
neither network nor userspace, so:

  - the /dev/kmsg stream to the PC never gets opened,
  - and the systemd watchdog (RuntimeWatchdogSec) does not exist yet, because
    PID 1 arms it and PID 1 has not started.

Result: the phone stays down until a person holds the button for 10-12 s.
Verified on 2026-08-15: with RuntimeWatchdogSec=60 set, a "does not
re-enumerate" hang does NOT recover on its own; one after systemd does.

WHAT IS ALREADY THERE
---------------------
The driver ships exactly the mechanism needed, but conditioned:

    if (qcom_wdt_is_running(&wdt->wdd)) {
            qcom_wdt_start(&wdt->wdd);
            set_bit(WDOG_HW_RUNNING, &wdt->wdd.status);
    }

With WDOG_HW_RUNNING set and CONFIG_WATCHDOG_HANDLE_BOOT_ENABLED=y (which this
kernel has), the watchdog core feeds the dog from a thread of its own until
userspace opens /dev/watchdog. If the kernel hangs, that thread stops feeding
and the hardware resets.

But U-Boot does not leave the watchdog running, so qcom_wdt_is_running() returns
false and it never gets enabled.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------
Drop the condition: always start it. From the kernel's first second until
systemd takes /dev/watchdog, the dog is alive and the core feeds it. A hang in
that window turns into an automatic reset within <=30 s.

Two benefits, and that is why this matters more than convenience:

  1. The phone stops needing a person to bring it back. That is what
     PENDIENTES.md points to as what makes it unusable as a daily driver.
  2. The watchdog reset is WARM, so the ramoops memory survives and the next
     boot can bring the trace of the hang, which is exactly what cannot be seen
     today for this failure mode.

Risk: if something legitimately kept the core from running its feeding thread
for more than 30 s, it would reset. On a normal boot that does not happen; and
if it did, that would in itself be the fault we are after.

--- a/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/qcom-wdt.c
@@ -285,15 +285,17 @@
 	watchdog_init_timeout(&wdt->wdd, 0, dev);
 
 	/*
-	 * If WDT is already running, call WDT start which
-	 * will stop the WDT, set timeouts as bootloader
-	 * might use different ones and set running bit
-	 * to inform the WDT subsystem to ping the WDT
+	 * Arrancarlo SIEMPRE, no solo si el gestor de arranque lo dejo puesto.
+	 *
+	 * U-Boot no lo deja encendido, asi que con la condicion original el
+	 * perro no existia hasta que systemd lo armaba, y los cuelgues del
+	 * arranque temprano -los mas frecuentes en el surya- se quedaban sin
+	 * nadie que resetease. Con WDOG_HW_RUNNING y
+	 * CONFIG_WATCHDOG_HANDLE_BOOT_ENABLED el nucleo lo alimenta desde aqui
+	 * hasta que userspace abra /dev/watchdog.
 	 */
-	if (qcom_wdt_is_running(&wdt->wdd)) {
-		qcom_wdt_start(&wdt->wdd);
-		set_bit(WDOG_HW_RUNNING, &wdt->wdd.status);
-	}
+	qcom_wdt_start(&wdt->wdd);
+	set_bit(WDOG_HW_RUNNING, &wdt->wdd.status);
 
 	ret = devm_watchdog_register_device(dev, &wdt->wdd);
 	if (ret)
